1
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:16,000
The terrifying motion picture from the terrifying No. 1 best seller.

2
00:10:20,000 --> 00:10:26,000
Jaws is a horror, thriller, adventure film directed by Steven Spielberg.

3
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:36,000
When the seaside community of Amity finds itself under attack by a dangerous great white shark, the town's chief of police, a young
marine biologist, and a grizzled shark hunter embark on a desperate quest to kill the beast before it strikes again.

4
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:46,000
Brody, played by Roy Scheider.

5
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:56,000
Quint, played by Robert Shaw.

6
00:31:00,000 --> 00:31:06,000
Hooper, played by Richard Dreyfuss.

7
00:36:10,000 --> 00:36:16,000
Ellen Brody, played by Lorraine Gary.

8
00:41:20,000 --> 00:41:26,000
Vaughn, played by Murray Hamilton.

9
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:36,000
Meadows, played by Carl Gottlieb.

10
00:51:40,000 --> 00:51:46,000
Jaws is a 1975 American  thriller film directed by Steven Spielberg.

11
00:56:50,000 --> 00:56:56,000
Based on the 1974 novel by Peter Benchley, it stars Roy Scheider as police chief Martin Brody, who, with the help of a marine biologist (Richard Dreyfuss) and
a professional shark hunter (Robert Shaw), hunts a man-eating great white shark that attacks beachgoers at a New England summer resort town.

12
01:02:00,000 --> 01:02:06,000
Murray Hamilton plays the town's mayor, and Lorraine Gary portrays Brody's wife.

13
01:07:10,000 --> 01:07:16,000
The screenplay is credited to Benchley, who wrote the first drafts, and actor-writer
Carl Gottlieb, who rewrote the script during principal photography.

14
01:12:20,000 --> 01:12:26,000
Shot mostly on location at Martha's Vineyard in Massachusetts from May to October 1974, Jaws was the first major motion
picture to be shot on the ocean and consequently had a troubled production, going over budget and schedule.

15
01:17:30,000 --> 01:17:36,000
As the art department's mechanical sharks often malfunctioned, Spielberg decided to mostly suggest the shark's presence,
employing an ominous and minimalist theme created by composer John Williams to indicate its impending appearances.

16
01:22:40,000 --> 01:22:46,000
Spielberg and others have compared this suggestive
approach to that of director Alfred Hitchcock.

17
01:27:50,000 --> 01:27:56,000
Universal Pictures released the film to over 450 screens, an exceptionally wide release for a major studio picture at the time,
accompanied by an extensive marketing campaign with heavy emphasis on television spots and tie-in merchandise.

18
01:33:00,000 --> 01:33:06,000
Regarded as a turning point in motion picture history, Jaws was the prototypical
summer blockbuster and won several awards for its music and editing.

19
01:38:10,000 --> 01:38:16,000
It was the highest-grossing film in history until the release of Star Wars two years later; both films were pivotal in establishing the modern Hollywood business model, which pursues
high box-office returns from action and adventure films with simple high-concept premises, released during the summer in thousands of theaters and advertised heavily.

20
01:43:20,000 --> 01:43:26,000
Jaws was followed by three sequels, none of which involved Spielberg
or Benchley, as well as many imitative thrillers.

21
01:48:30,000 --> 01:48:36,000
In 2001, the Library of Congress selected it for preservation
in the United States National Film Registry.

22
01:53:40,000 --> 01:53:46,000
The film explores themes of dying and death, beach,
based on novel or book, bathing, shipwreck.

23
01:58:50,000 --> 01:58:56,000
The tone is dark and intense.
